---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<BaseLayout title="Production Supplies & Replenishment" description="An inventory planning system that turns weekly physical counts into reorder decisions, supply coverage, purchasing visibility, and forward-looking requirements.">
<section class="case-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <a class="case-back" href="/built-systems/">← Built Systems</a>
    <div class="case-kicker">03 / Inventory Operations</div>
    <h1>Production Supplies &<br/><em>Replenishment</em></h1>
    <p class="case-deck">A working inventory system for managing recurring production supplies: what is on hand, what needs to be ordered, what is already coming, and what future demand is likely to require.</p>
    <div class="case-role-line"><span>Inventory Planning</span><span>Replenishment Logic</span><span>Supplier Visibility</span><span>Cost Tracking</span><span>Forecasting</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-metrics case-metrics-dark">
  <div class="container case-metric-grid">
    <div><strong>~$300K</strong><span>Inventory Actively Managed</span></div>
    <div><strong>60–65</strong><span>Recurring Supply Items</span></div>
    <div><strong>~$40K</strong><span>Excess Inventory Reconciled</span></div>
    <div><strong>Weekly</strong><span>Physical Count Cycle</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="container case-two-col">
    <div>
      <div class="case-section-no">01</div><div class="eyebrow">The Starting Point</div>
      <h2>A count sheet could show what was there. Ordering still depended on local knowledge.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-copy">
      <p>The existing inventory sheet was primarily used to confirm physical and consignment counts. It could show the item, quantity, location, and cost, but it did not provide the supplier, lead time, reorder point, or purchasing history needed to decide what should happen next.</p>
      <p>That left a large part of replenishment dependent on departmental knowledge and repeated searches through prior requisitions. The work area had also experienced shortages of essential supplies as well as excess purchases that remained unused for years.</p>
      <p class="case-emphasis">I turned the weekly count into an operating system for replenishment rather than a record of inventory alone.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt section-divider inventory-model-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">02</div><div class="eyebrow">Inventory Model</div>
    <h2>Put the information needed to reorder an item in one place.</h2>
    <div class="inventory-field-grid">
      <span>Supply ID</span><span>Item</span><span>Type</span><span>Location</span><span>Supplier</span>
      <span>Quantity on Hand</span><span>Unit Cost</span><span>Reorder Level</span><span>Lead Time</span><span>Open PO / REQ</span>
      <span>Committed Delivery</span><span>Order Notes / MOQ</span>
    </div>
    <div class="inventory-flow">
      <div><small>01</small><strong>Physical Count</strong><span>Update current stock</span></div><b>→</b>
      <div><small>02</small><strong>Stock Position</strong><span>Compare to reorder level</span></div><b>→</b>
      <div><small>03</small><strong>Open Orders</strong><span>Account for incoming supply</span></div><b>→</b>
      <div><small>04</small><strong>Purchase Decision</strong><span>Order, monitor, or hold</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section inventory-demo-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">03</div><div class="eyebrow">Portfolio Demo</div>
    <h2>A weekly action view, rebuilt for a fictional fashion and beauty operation.</h2>
    <p class="case-intro">The original system manages production consumables in manufacturing. This portfolio version uses synthetic lifestyle-brand products, supplies, vendors, costs, and purchase activity to demonstrate the same planning logic in a different industry context.</p>

    <div class="inventory-demo-window">
      <div class="inventory-demo-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><b>Inventory Operations Hub</b></div>
      <div class="inventory-demo-body">
        <div class="inventory-demo-title"><small>WEEKLY ACTION VIEW</small><strong>Inventory, ordering, and forecast risk in one place</strong></div>
        <div class="inventory-kpis">
          <div><span>Inventory Value</span><strong>$286K</strong></div>
          <div><span>Order Now</span><strong>8</strong></div>
          <div><span>Order Placed</span><strong>4</strong></div>
          <div><span>Late PO</span><strong>2</strong></div>
          <div><span>Forecast Short</span><strong>11</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="inventory-dashboard-grid">
          <div class="inventory-status-panel">
            <div class="inventory-panel-head"><strong>Ordering Status</strong><span>Current inventory</span></div>
            <div class="status-line"><span>Order Now</span><i style="--w:78%"></i><b>8</b></div>
            <div class="status-line"><span>Order Placed</span><i style="--w:42%"></i><b>4</b></div>
            <div class="status-line"><span>Open PO</span><i style="--w:58%"></i><b>6</b></div>
            <div class="status-line"><span>Due Soon</span><i style="--w:30%"></i><b>3</b></div>
          </div>
          <div class="forecast-risk-panel">
            <div class="inventory-panel-head"><strong>Forecast Risk by Supply Type</strong><span>Quarter outlook</span></div>
            <div class="risk-table-head"><span>Type</span><span>Short</span><span>Watch</span><span>OK</span></div>
            <div class="risk-table-row"><span>Components</span><b>9</b><em>1</em><i>2</i></div>
            <div class="risk-table-row"><span>Packaging</span><b>3</b><em>2</em><i>3</i></div>
            <div class="risk-table-row"><span>Materials</span><b>4</b><em>1</em><i>5</i></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="inventory-action-queue">
          <div class="inventory-panel-head"><strong>Action Queue — Items Requiring Review</strong><span>Non-OK inventory</span></div>
          <div class="action-head"><span>Status</span><span>Supply</span><span>Qty on Hand</span><span>Reorder Level</span><span>Open Order</span><span>Next Order</span></div>
          <div class="action-row"><em>ORDER NOW</em><span>Packaging component</span><span>420</span><span>800</span><span>—</span><span>Now</span></div>
          <div class="action-row"><em>LATE PO</em><span>Core material</span><span>575</span><span>500</span><span>100</span><span>Review</span></div>
          <div class="action-row"><em>DUE SOON</em><span>Product component</span><span>1,350</span><span>1,500</span><span>500</span><span>12 days</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">The interface above is a simplified website representation of the downloadable workbook. All demo data is synthetic.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt inventory-forecast-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">04</div><div class="eyebrow">Forward Planning</div>
    <h2>Current stock is only half the question.</h2>
    <div class="forecast-logic">
      <div class="forecast-source">
        <small>Quarterly Product Forecast</small>
        <strong>Product + planned units</strong>
        <span>Product setup links each SKU to its materials, components, packaging, and current supply costs.</span>
      </div>
      <b>→</b>
      <div class="forecast-source">
        <small>Supply Requirement</small>
        <strong>Forecast demand by supply</strong>
        <span>Requirements are consolidated across products and compared with inventory plus open purchase orders.</span>
      </div>
      <b>→</b>
      <div class="forecast-source">
        <small>Planning Flag</small>
        <strong>Short · Watch · OK</strong>
        <span>The output identifies which supplies need attention before forecast demand reaches the floor.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="forecast-demo-table">
      <div class="forecast-table-head"><span>Supply</span><span>Forecast Requirement</span><span>On Hand</span><span>Open Order</span><span>Net After Forecast</span><span>Flag</span></div>
      <div class="forecast-table-row"><span>Component A</span><span>1,200</span><span>800</span><span>0</span><span>-400</span><b>SHORT</b></div>
      <div class="forecast-table-row"><span>Component B</span><span>3,000</span><span>3,449</span><span>100</span><span>549</span><em>WATCH</em></div>
      <div class="forecast-table-row"><span>Material C</span><span>720</span><span>1,600</span><span>0</span><span>880</span><i>OK</i></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section inventory-cost-section">
  <div class="container inventory-cost-grid">
    <div>
      <div class="case-section-no">05</div><div class="eyebrow">Cost & Inventory Quality</div>
      <h2>Replenishment also created a better way to see what should no longer be there.</h2>
      <p>With item-level cost and purchasing information in one place, the system made it easier to review year-over-year price changes, duplicate purchasing risk, and inventory that had remained on hand without being used.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cost-history-card">
      <div><small>2024</small><strong>$0.33</strong></div><span>→</span><div><small>2025</small><strong>$0.36</strong></div><span>→</span><div><small>2026</small><strong>$0.38</strong></div>
      <p>Annual cost history provides a simple reference for supply-price movement while current operating prices remain in the supply master.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-outcomes inventory-outcomes">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no light">06</div><div class="eyebrow light">Operational Results</div>
    <h2>Better visibility changed both shortage prevention and cleanup.</h2>
    <div class="outcome-grid">
      <div><strong>~$300K</strong><span>Inventory Under Active Management</span></div>
      <div><strong>~$40K</strong><span>Excess Inventory Identified & Reconciled</span></div>
      <div><strong>3–5</strong><span>Material Shortages Avoided</span></div>
      <div><strong>Reduced</strong><span>Duplicate Purchasing</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt inventory-next-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">07</div><div class="eyebrow">Next Iteration</div>
    <h2>Move from manual replenishment support toward connected inventory planning.</h2>
    <div class="roadmap inventory-roadmap">
      <span>Automated Reorder Alerts</span><span>Consumption Forecasting</span><span>Barcode Scanning</span><span>ERP / Product-BOM Integration</span>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">The current system is maintained through weekly physical counts and purchasing updates. The portfolio demo extends the original concept with product-driven quarterly supply requirements to show how the same data model can support more forward-looking planning.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section inventory-download">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="download-card">
      <div><div class="eyebrow">Explore the Demo</div><h2>Fashion & Beauty Inventory Operations</h2>
      <p>Download the working Excel portfolio demo with inventory tracking, supply and product masters, quarterly forecasting, supply requirements, vendor reference, cost history, dashboard, and operating instructions.</p></div>
      <a href={`${base}/artifacts/inventory-replenishment-portfolio-demo.xlsx`} download><span>Download Excel Demo</span><b>↓</b></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-next">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Next System</span>
    <a href="/built-systems/workforce-capability-planning/">
      <strong>Workforce Capability Planning System</strong><span>→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
